# A backup that happens whether or not anyone remembers.
#
# Install on the host as the deploy user:
#
#   crontab -e
#
# and paste the two lines below, adjusting the path. The second line is the one
# people leave out, and it is the one that matters: a backup nobody has restored is
# a hypothesis, so the weekly drill restores the newest backup into a scratch
# database, checks the row counts and the constraints, and drops it. It fails loudly
# rather than silently producing dumps that cannot be loaded.
#
# Twice a day rather than nightly. Data time here runs at 720x, so a day of wall
# clock is roughly two years of simulated readings - losing "only" twelve hours of
# it is losing more than it sounds.

SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin

# Backup at 03:20 and 15:20. Keeps the last 7 by default (GEMP_BACKUP_KEEP).
20 3,15 * * *   cd /srv/gemp && ./infra/deploy/backup.sh >> .deploy/backup.log 2>&1

# Restore drill, Sundays at 04:10. Non-destructive: it uses a scratch database.
10 4 * * 0      cd /srv/gemp && d=$(ls -d .deploy/backups/*/ | tail -1) && ./infra/deploy/restore.sh "$d" --into gemp_restore_test >> .deploy/backup.log 2>&1

# Somewhere other than this disk.
#
# Both lines above write to .deploy/backups, which is on the same disk as the
# database they are backing up. That protects against the database being corrupted,
# dropped or restored badly. It does NOT protect against the disk failing, the
# laptop being lost, or the volume being deleted - and one of those is the scenario
# that actually ends the project.
#
# Pass a destination on other media and the same script writes there:
#
#   20 3,15 * * *  cd /srv/gemp && ./infra/deploy/backup.sh /mnt/backup/gemp >> .deploy/backup.log 2>&1
#
# For the demonstration specifically: take one backup onto a USB stick the day
# before, verify it with `restore.sh <dir> --into gemp_restore_test`, and carry it
# separately from the laptop.
